direct-once
owner: fable-5.1 session 53f400ad
started: 2026-09-16
what: the operator's ask (2026-09-16) after the call-by-need question:
  `Free.once(p)` (also `!.once`), a program that runs p at most once
  and answers from its cell after — the by-need cousin of `Delay`,
  which is by-name (forced every time the loop reaches it). In a
  direct block `lazy val x = !p` is that word: the rhs becomes a once
  handle, every use of x a bind at the use site, so the effect runs
  at the FIRST demand and never again — currently a refusal ("in a
  lazy val"). A row that names Choose (the library's multi-shot
  effect) refuses the sugar unless `import okay.Direct.onceAcrossShots.given`
  is in scope, the eagerCalls pattern: with it the cell keeps its
  once semantics across branches, by the user's decision. Re-entrance
  (a knot, a concurrent force, an abandoned first run) is a loud
  error, not a second run.
files: src/main/scala/Free.scala, src/main/scala/Direct.scala,
